<div id="shopify-section-sections--19674847871158__header" class="vy-vy-vy-shopify-section vy-vy-vy-shopify-section-group-header-group vy-vy-vy-section-header"> <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="vy-vy-vy-hidden"><use href="#vsym-20" xlink:href="#vsym-20"></use></svg> </div> <main id="MainContent" class="vy-vy-vy-content-for-layout vy-vy-vy-focus-none" role="main" tabindex="-1"> <div id="replo-fullpage-element" data-runtime-version="daefba70-f888-11f0-bde4-23589a709ef8" style="display: block !important" data-alchemy-element-root="65aa4fd9-9605-4f13-ad87-8e02eadd644d"> <div data-alchemy-element-mount-node="true"><div class="vy-vy-vy-alchemy__element vy-vy-vy-alchemy-reset vy-vy-vy-overflow-clip"><div data-rid="8cf2f58b-a2e3-46f7-b728-73a457a025f4" class="vy-vy-vy-r-a9c4zn"><div data-rid="e4549197-1b9c-4e1c-912d-c642aa52ca07" class="vy-vy-vy-r-z0jkw8">
<div data-rid="87b03e09-c60f-47d2-bff5-5b7f9d9b0935" class="vy-vy-vy-r-kbnevj">
<div data-rid="4d739a2d-1b2a-4992-b74b-67f333fbae8f" class="vy-vy-vy-r-1p6kmfd">
<!-- breadcrumb removed -->
<div data-rid="b363fe84-8f40-46c4-b3db-af0371cf2201" class="vy-vy-vy-r-ijaxxl">
<div data-rid="19b5ea51-d876-4c97-8bac-11997080d67b" class="vy-vy-vy-r-73kd2d vy-vy-vy-alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><h1>At 68, I decided it was time to start looking after my lungs. Now a herb people have leaned on for their lungs for centuries is part of my morning, just two droppers in a glass of water, and it's the easiest thing I do all day.</h1></span></div>
<div data-rid="5f91f721-34ae-4c63-984e-b662f2139423" class="vy-vy-vy-r-t9cuvq vy-vy-vy-alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>For years I never gave my lungs a second thought. <mark data-color="#e9f5fcFF" style="background-color: #e9f5fcFF; color: inherit">Then Carol told me about a herb people have long relied on for lung support: two droppers in a glass of water.</mark> Now that little bottle sits by my kettle, and my day starts with it.</p></span></div>
<div class="vy-author-byline" style="display:flex !important;align-items:center;gap:12px;margin:22px 0 4px;">
<img src="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-046-betterbrand/images/author.jpg" alt="Eleanor Hayes" style="width:56px !important;height:56px !important;min-width:56px !important;max-width:56px !important;border-radius:50% !important;object-fit:cover !important;flex:0 0 56px !important;display:block !important;box-shadow:0 1px 4px rgba(0,0,0,.12);">
<span style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;line-height:1.35;min-width:0;">
<span style="font-weight:700;font-size:15px;color:#1a1a1a;white-space:nowrap;">Eleanor Hayes</span>
<span style="font-size:13px;color:#6f6357;white-space:nowrap;">as told to Natural Health Digest</span>
</span>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div data-rid="3428a382-d4a2-46d4-aeb1-5e9f10595452" class="vy-vy-vy-r-6ar4dq"><picture data-rid="07254baf-13c1-4f73-b9d1-da0c977cdd7a" class="vy-vy-vy-r-7nqvm4"><source srcset="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-046-betterbrand/images/img-3.jpg" media="(max-width: 640px)"><source srcset="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-046-betterbrand/images/img-3.jpg" media="(min-width: 641px) and (max-width: 1024px)"><source srcset="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-046-betterbrand/images/img-3.jpg" media="(min-width: 1025px) and (max-width: 2400px)"><img src="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-046-betterbrand/images/img-3.jpg" class="vy-vy-vy-r-wgidcy" loading="eager"></picture></div>
<div data-rid="1d5b2839-8135-41b1-9169-5384832138a3" class="vy-vy-vy-r-scmfee"><div data-rid="3fe0a971-08e0-432b-8569-74f0ac0e2100" class="vy-vy-vy-r-12357h vy-vy-vy-alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p><strong>I'm 68, and I can tell you the exact morning I decided to start looking after my lungs. I woke before six, filled the kettle as always, and thought: my lungs work every minute of every day, and I have never once done a single thing to support them.</strong></p>
<p><br><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)">It's not that anything was wrong. I ate reasonably, I stayed active, I kept up with my check-ups. But there was one thing I had simply never thought about: my lungs. They did their job quietly in the background, and I never gave them a second thought or a scrap of support.</span><br><br></p>
<p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)">I mentioned it to my sister, that I'd been thinking I ought to do something for my lungs at our age. She shrugged and said what most people say: nobody really thinks about their lungs, you just breathe and get on with it.</span><br><br></p>
<p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)"><strong>So I let it go for a while. But the thought kept coming back. I look after my skin, my teeth, my heart, I thought, and yet the organs that work every single minute of the day get nothing from me at all. It started to feel like an obvious gap.</strong></span><br><br></p>
<p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)">The more I read, the more it stuck with me. Our lungs do quiet, constant work for us our whole lives, and most of us never think to give them any support at all. I decided I didn't want to be one more person who left it to chance.</span><br><br></p>
<p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)">Then I met Carol for coffee. We've been friends for thirty years. She stopped working a couple of years before I did, practical and funny, the sort who researches everything before she buys it. I mentioned I'd been wanting to do something to look after my lungs, and she put her cup down and smiled, because it turned out she'd been doing exactly that for a year.</span><br><br></p>
<p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)">"Funny you should say that," she said, stirring her coffee. "I started doing something for mine last winter."</span><br><br></p>
<p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)">I told her what I'd been reading, and what my sister had said, that nobody really bothers about their lungs.</span><br><br></p>
<p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)">"I just want to actually do something for them," I said. "Everyone talks about looking after your heart or your joints, but nobody ever mentions the lungs. I don't even know where you'd start."</span><br><br></p>
<p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)">She set her cup down and nodded, like she'd asked herself the very same question a year ago. Then she said the thing that stuck:</span><br><br></p>
<p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)">"I was exactly where you are last winter, meaning to look after my lungs and never actually doing it. Then I started mullein drops. It's the simplest habit in the world. Give it a couple of weeks and you'll see how easy it is."</span></p></span></div></div>
<div data-rid="f9ed0282-e79d-4b58-869c-4c65411c8295" class="vy-vy-vy-r-1o66a95">
<div data-rid="6514d256-32c8-41c8-a2d4-2498a6c46944" class="vy-vy-vy-r-48xodv"><div data-rid="3b7f3f34-290f-4e0c-bb83-b1c4c14ab7f3" class="vy-vy-vy-r-1assrdo vy-vy-vy-alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><h2>The coffee where Carol told me about mullein</h2></span></div></div>
<div data-rid="102db264-f288-4e13-9fb7-a1979fea3774" class="vy-vy-vy-r-1utup6c"><picture data-rid="db2bbd37-1ae9-4deb-b493-8379e1525ccb" class="vy-vy-vy-r-vmdqyv"><source srcset="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-046-betterbrand/images/img-4.jpg" media="(max-width: 640px)"><source srcset="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-046-betterbrand/images/img-4.jpg" media="(min-width: 641px) and (max-width: 1024px)"><source srcset="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-046-betterbrand/images/img-4.jpg" media="(min-width: 1025px) and (max-width: 2400px)"><img src="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-046-betterbrand/images/img-4.jpg" alt="Two friends talking over coffee about mullein drops" class="vy-vy-vy-r-9vp9sy" loading="eager"></picture></div>
<div data-rid="416b4835-b77b-4e31-be11-5b8d5eb6e2fb" class="vy-vy-vy-r-103iwmh"><div data-rid="992cbe25-88fb-49a4-b711-6f3fff67feac" class="vy-vy-vy-r-5ybhrv vy-vy-vy-alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Carol pulled out her phone right there at the table and showed me the page. BetterLungs Mullein + Chlorophyll, drops in a small amber bottle. Mullein, she said, is the herb people have relied on for lung support for centuries, all the way back to Greek and Roman medicine, and somewhere along the line most of us just forgot it existed.<br><br></p>
<p>Then she read me the rest of the label. Mullein leaf extract for the lungs, chlorophyll for energy, purified water and vegetable glycerine and not much else. Formulated by a pharmacist, made in the USA, alcohol free. I went quiet for a moment.<br><br></p>
<p>"This is the one thing that's actually stuck," she said. "Two full droppers in a glass of water, first thing, and that's the whole routine. It tastes mild and a little green, nothing like medicine. <strong>No pills to swallow, no caffeine, just two droppers in your morning water.</strong>"<br><br></p>
<p>I looked at her. "Carol, it's an herb. My cabinet is full of things that were supposed to help."<br><br></p>
<p>She just smiled and kept scrolling. "Not like this one. Your great-grandmother would have recognized this herb. Somebody finally measured it properly and put it into a clean daily dose. Take it every morning and pay attention to the first week. <strong>You won't need me to talk you into ordering a second bottle.</strong>"<br><br></p>
<p>I was skeptical. I'd already worked my way through the usual things over the years, even a tea that tasted like lawn clippings, and I'd long stopped expecting much from a shiny product page.<br><br></p>
<p>But Carol had already sent me the link, so I ordered a bottle that same evening.</p></span></div></div>
</div>
<div data-rid="48c0f854-e5f2-4779-bb4b-55cf3d91f90e" class="vy-vy-vy-r-1r3euik">
<div data-rid="a5848c73-1ab7-4c3e-a2e1-9afff9e67c49" class="vy-vy-vy-r-12rzfy5"><div data-rid="0dca9c46-58f1-4559-983d-03765a3874ca" class="vy-vy-vy-r-l3hav7 vy-vy-vy-alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><h2>The first mornings with the little amber bottle</h2></span></div></div>
<div data-rid="9d62fcf2-265d-4b5c-82c6-d2388dfccd38" class="vy-vy-vy-r-1ph6ghk"><picture data-rid="348c8797-f352-4b23-ab18-9232ba342d87" class="vy-vy-vy-r-1lkr9e1"><source srcset="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-046-betterbrand/images/img-5.jpg" media="(max-width: 640px)"><source srcset="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-046-betterbrand/images/img-5.jpg" media="(min-width: 641px) and (max-width: 1024px)"><source srcset="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-046-betterbrand/images/img-5.jpg" media="(min-width: 1025px) and (max-width: 2400px)"><img src="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-046-betterbrand/images/img-5.jpg" class="vy-vy-vy-r-8o2viz" loading="eager"></picture></div>
<div data-rid="3f0384b0-ede1-4738-8f01-7b979c5cd77d" class="vy-vy-vy-r-aeqb59"><div data-rid="50bd4e06-3f8c-4da1-9719-0f816836291f" class="vy-vy-vy-r-1t1t72j vy-vy-vy-alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>The box came a few days later. A small amber glass bottle with a dropper top and a dark green label, lighter than I expected for something with that much history behind it. Alcohol free, thirty servings, a full month in one little bottle.<br><br></p>
<p>The label took about ten seconds to read. Mullein leaf extract, chlorophyll, purified water, vegetable glycerine, natural flavorings. Nothing dense, nothing mysterious. Just the herb Carol had told me about, in a form I could actually measure.<br><br></p>
<p>I braced for the usual story anyway: three faithful days, then one more bottle gathering dust next to the vitamins. Carol had said most people are surprised how quickly it becomes a habit they actually keep.<br><br></p>
<p>She was right. The first morning I squeezed two full droppers into 8 oz of water, gave it a stir and drank it with my toast. It went down easy, and drinking the whole glass is part of the point: it starts the morning with a little hydration and a moment for myself.<br><br></p>
<p>The next morning, the same. Kettle on, two droppers, the glass of water before anything else.<br><br></p>
<p>By the third morning it had already become automatic, the first thing I reached for after the kettle. No decision to make, no talking myself into it, just two droppers in a glass of water while the tea steeped.<br><br></p>
<p><strong>No pills to line up. No bitter aftertaste. Just one small ritual that quietly became the part of my morning I refused to skip.</strong><br><br></p>
<p>I texted Carol: "Three mornings in and I haven't forgotten it once. That never happens with me."<br><br></p>
<p>She texted back: "That's the whole point. <strong>It feels like a habit, not a chore.</strong> That's why people actually stick with it."<br><br></p>
<p>That night I sat on the edge of my bed for a minute, the bottle already set out on the counter for morning.<br><br></p>
<p>I felt... good about it. Not because of one glass of water, but because I'd finally started doing something for my lungs after years of meaning to.<br><br></p>
<p>The habit had <strong>stuck.</strong><br><br></p>
<p>I sang along with the radio while my tea steeped, glass in hand, the way I do now every morning. It had taken all of thirty seconds to add to my day, and I <strong>liked knowing I'd done it.</strong><br><br></p>
<p>I felt like a woman who'd finally started a good habit and meant to keep it.<br><br></p>
<p><strong>And that was DAY THREE.</strong></p></span></div></div>
</div>
<div data-rid="0f79d102-7685-427a-97f7-384a156284fb" class="vy-vy-vy-r-1i5xl6o">
<div data-rid="5df61287-3519-4c5d-ad58-2c1e2a620960" class="vy-vy-vy-r-w8aq40"><div data-rid="aaf0257d-e3ab-403e-8873-4a95c1b96c14" class="vy-vy-vy-r-aifh4o vy-vy-vy-alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><h2>Why one simple herb earned a place in my mornings</h2></span></div></div>
<div data-rid="29bbe301-0046-4fcd-a683-4f1d88ff5a93" class="vy-vy-vy-r-g2lqx3"><picture data-rid="55f62894-04e3-4e79-8438-b1c51a597af6" class="vy-vy-vy-r-1pahr4a"><source srcset="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-046-betterbrand/images/img-6.jpg" media="(max-width: 640px)"><source srcset="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-046-betterbrand/images/img-6.jpg" media="(min-width: 641px) and (max-width: 1024px)"><source srcset="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-046-betterbrand/images/img-6.jpg" media="(min-width: 1025px) and (max-width: 2400px)"><img src="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-046-betterbrand/images/img-6.jpg" alt="A relaxed woman at her kitchen table with a mug of tea and her morning glass of water" class="vy-vy-vy-r-1w41bw4" loading="eager"></picture></div>
<div data-rid="a0de0f9b-1c35-410e-92c7-6f088c2f7ea9" class="vy-vy-vy-r-13zo5va">
<div data-rid="8973131b-19ac-49fa-b7c1-f548594cda1b" class="vy-vy-vy-r-x20i5p vy-vy-vy-alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>After a couple of weeks I wanted to understand why one old herb had become the part of my morning I looked forward to. I called Carol and made her explain the whole thing.<br><br></p>
<p>What she told me honestly made me wish I'd known about it years earlier, because it is such a simple thing to add to a morning.</p></span></div>
<div data-rid="8dc0fcbd-8ca6-4b7d-9931-11042c6df7b9" class="vy-vy-vy-r-13v77ik">
<div data-rid="e951b7e3-6f6b-40fc-a0ae-6861e4f80c0d" class="vy-vy-vy-r-1kr1ha8 vy-vy-vy-alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><h3>A centuries-old herb for daily lung support</h3></span></div>
<div data-rid="04f020c6-c4be-47db-b602-d5b222bf892d" class="vy-vy-vy-r-1az2u1r vy-vy-vy-alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p><strong>Mullein is a plant people have leaned on for lung support for centuries, going all the way back to Greek and Roman medicine. It is rich in antioxidants, and taken daily it gives the lungs steady, gentle support, morning after morning.</strong><br><br></p>
<p>What I liked was how uncomplicated it is. Two droppers in a glass of water each morning, no routine to manage and nothing to fuss over. It turns daily lung support into something as easy as drinking a glass of water.<br><br></p>
